<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!--
        V1 BASELINE for the PAYMENTS report generator in dcre_pay (owner directive
        2026-08-08: every DCRE database is dropped and cut over directly, so there is
        no historic state to migrate and no deployed schema to protect).

        This is NOT a port of CRG's 53-changeset history. Each object is declared once
        in its final shape, and the re-run guards that history required are absent on
        purpose. DATABASECHANGELOG is what makes a re-run safe from a clean baseline;
        the guards CRG carried (validCheckSum ANY, MARK_RAN preconditions over
        pre-existing objects, IF-NOT-EXISTS on CREATEs) existed to survive a migration
        that has now been abandoned, and reproducing them would be exactly the
        hacking-around-Liquibase the owner has forbidden. There are now NO exceptions:
        the batch metadata was the last one and became typed tags on 2026-08-08.

        ============================ THE ORDERING CONTRACT ============================

        PRG READS nine relations it does not own, and creates none of them:

          tx_header, tx_entry ........................... PRR
          validation_log ................................ PTV
          isr_resp / sbsr_resp / pbsr_resp .............. PIX / PSX / PPX
          prw_emission_group / prw_emission /
          prw_emission_member ........................... PRW

        Until 2026-08-08 this changelog PRE-CREATED all nine behind onFail="CONTINUE"
        preconditions, because PRG is clock-launched and can migrate on a fresh dcre_pay
        before any of those services has ever run. CRG carried the identical guard, and
        retired it; PRG was forked from CRG before that retirement and inherited the
        anti-pattern without the fix. Two things were wrong with it:

          - it made PRG a SECOND writer of nine relations it does not own. CONTINUE
            skips SILENTLY, so whichever service migrated second inherited the other's
            shape permanently and invisibly, and in the collections twin the mint had
            already drifted from its owner;
          - a PRG mint winning the race would crashloop the OWNER, whose own baseline
            creates these tables unguarded. The guard prevented one failure by causing
            a worse one.

        WHAT GUARANTEES THE RELATIONS EXIST NOW, per destination:

          - 002's read-path indexes and 003's views both name relations by identifier,
            and CockroachDB resolves a view body at CREATE time. If PRG migrates first,
            this changelog FAILS, names the missing relation, and leaves its changesets
            unapplied. The pod restarts, retries, and converges the moment the owner has
            migrated. That is loud, self-healing and visible in pod status.
          - it is NOT the silent-zero shape (A-76, A-79). A missing relation cannot
            produce an empty result here, because the object is never created at all;
            there is no view to query and return nothing. The failure this changelog can
            have is a crash, and a crash is a bad outcome with a good property, which is
            that somebody finds out. A MARK_RAN precondition WOULD reintroduce the silent
            shape by recording the skip permanently, which is why there is none.
          - PRG's own integration tests need the same relations, and get them from
            src/test/resources/db/changelog/test/001-read-sources.xml via
            db.changelog-test-master.xml, which runs the fixture and then THIS master by
            its production classpath path, so every production changeset keeps its exact
            identity and the tests exercise the artifact that ships.
          - the fixture is a hand-maintained mirror of other repositories' DDL, which is
            a drift class. BootstrapSourceParityTest reads the OWNERS' changelogs off
            disk and compares them column by column, so the mirror is a checked fact
            rather than a remembered one.

        Order is load-bearing: owned tables (the views reference prg_status_class and
        prg_delivery_ledger), then views, then batch metadata.
    -->
    <include file="db/changelog/2026/08/db.changelog-2026-08.xml"/>

    <!-- 2026/09 carries REPAIRS, and runs LAST for that reason: each of its changesets is
         written against the state 2026/08 leaves behind, and reads the shape of the object it
         repairs rather than trusting the name. See the file itself for which database states
         it converges. -->
    <include file="db/changelog/2026/09/db.changelog-2026-09.xml"/>

</databaseChangeLog>
